    <content>
      <Value><![CDATA[## The Modern Web is Heavy

Most websites ship **megabytes** of JavaScript:

- React: ~40 KB (gzipped)
- Vue: ~32 KB (gzipped)
- Bootstrap: ~22 KB (gzipped)
- jQuery: ~30 KB (gzipped)

Add them together, plus all the plugins, utilities, and polyfills, and you're pushing hundreds of KB—sometimes **megabytes**—before users see content.

This site? **Zero JavaScript frameworks.**

### What I Shipped Instead

**Two CSS files:**
1. `neon-cyberpunk.css` (~5 KB)
2. `blog.css` (~7 KB)

**Total:** ~12 KB of styling. That's it.

No React. No Vue. No Bootstrap. No jQuery. No build tools. No bundlers.

Just **semantic HTML** and **pure CSS**.

### Why This Matters

**Speed:** The site loads instantly. No framework initialization. No hydration. No bundle parsing.

**Simplicity:** When something breaks, I know exactly where to look. No debugging through layers of abstractions.

**Accessibility:** Semantic HTML works with screen readers, keyboard navigation, and assistive technologies out of the box.

### What I Achieved Without Frameworks

- **Animated backgrounds** (CSS keyframes)
- **Glitch effects** (CSS transforms + filters)
- **Hover interactions** (CSS :hover pseudo-class)
- **Responsive layouts** (CSS Grid + Flexbox)
- **Neon glow effects** (CSS box-shadow + text-shadow)

All native. All standard. All performant.

### The Trade-Off

Frameworks give you:
- Rapid prototyping
- Rich interactions
- State management
- Component reusability

For a **blog**? I don't need any of that. Blogs are mostly static content. They don't need complex state or interactivity.

### The Verdict

By staying framework-free, I built a site that:
- Loads in milliseconds
- Works on any device
- Requires no build step
- Will never have "dependency hell"

**Sometimes the best tool is no tool at all.**

Just standards. Just simplicity. Just the web.]]></Value>
